// ---------- WRITER_MODE=story ----------
export const STORY_PLAN_TASK = "Plan the spoken retelling of this chapter for a recap channel. You are the story\neditor, not a narrator and not a picture editor. You know where the arc is going, so\nsetup gets weight and filler gets cut. Read everything first, then SELECT; never walk.\n\nSOURCE EVENTS AND CAST:\n{source}\nORIGINAL SOURCE LOOKUP (captions are fallible; source dialogue establishes history):\n{transcript}\nSTORY SO FAR (series memory; continuity only, never new facts for this chapter):\n{so_far}\nEARLIER NARRATION (continuity, not new facts):\n{previous}\nALREADY SPOKEN OPENING:\n{opening}\n{upcoming}\nSELECTION:\n- Keep only moments that change the situation: a want, an obstacle, a choice, a\n  reveal, a reversal, a cost. Per scene keep the 3-6 that carry it. A whole fight is\n  about six moments: the charge, the clash, the turn, the reaction that matters, the\n  exhaustion or cost, the outcome.\n- Drop filler freely, with no reason needed: walking, connecting gestures, repeated\n  reactions, small talk, a beat that restates the last one. Merge micro-events into\n  the turn they serve. omitted_events is optional.\n- If an UPCOMING block is present, use it only to weigh what you keep: setup that\n  pays off later earns a moment, a dead end does not. Nothing from it may enter any field.\n- Moments are in TELLING order. A time jump is ONE moment carrying the source's interval.\n{shape_plan}\n- When consecutive kept moments happen in different places or times, say so at the\n  start of the later moment's detail (\"later that night, at the restaurant: ...\").\n  Never merge moments from different places or times into one moment.\n- Never re-plan what the viewer already heard in EARLIER NARRATION.\n\nGROUNDING (these never bend for pace):\n- Only source-established information. Never invent a cause, timeline, motive or\n  outcome. Distinguish self-blame from objective guilt, a future preview from current\n  action, and what the narrator knows from what a character knows yet.\n- Cast roles/actions are fallible summaries, not proof of an event or duration. A\n  historical figure, elapsed time, skill origin or recognition must be established by\n  the chapter's dialogue/world text or a directly described event; otherwise omit it.\n- Reader event/visual summaries can misread a sequence. Adjacent images of danger,\n  rescue and aftermath are NOT two deaths or an unseen trapped person. Do not invent\n  an offscreen location or a separate flashback.\n- Preserve relative chronology: months AFTER a milestone never becomes the SAME DAY.\n  Read adjacent dialogue fragments together and carry the explicit interval.\n- Attribute reported/offscreen mechanisms as a character's account; never turn a\n  text-only report into a visibly depicted action.\n- No panel IDs, bubble transcripts, captions, clothing, framing or proposed images.\n\nReply STRICT JSON only:\n{{\"premise\":\"the chapter-level predicament/reversal in one or two sentences\",\n\"turns\":[{{\"want\":\"grounded desire\",\"obstacle\":\"grounded obstacle\",\n\"choice\":\"grounded decision/revelation\",\"change\":\"grounded consequence\"}}],\n\"moments\":[{{\"event\":\"one situation-changing moment\",\"source_events\":[1],\n\"evidence_type\":\"visible or reported or internal\",\n\"detail\":\"source-grounded specifics needed to tell it: who, what changed, the line that matters\"}}],\n\"omitted_events\":[{{\"event\":2,\"reason\":\"optional note\"}}]}}\nCausal fields may be empty when unsupported. omitted_events may be [] or absent.\n";

export const STORY_TASK = "Retell this chapter to a friend, the way a top manhwa recap channel does: a fast,\nwarm, connected story that keeps the viewer hooked. The viewer sees the pictures;\nyou tell them what HAPPENS, why, and what it costs. Never describe the image.\n\nSTORY PLAN AND CAST (selected moments in telling order; facts, not a checklist):\n{source}\nSTORY SO FAR (series memory; continuity only, never new facts):\n{so_far}\nEARLIER NARRATION (voice/continuity only, not this chapter's factual source):\n{previous}\nALREADY SPOKEN OPENING, if supplied (continue it; do not repeat it):\n{opening}\n\nVoice and shape:\n- Retelling, not description. Every line moves plot, motive or consequence. If\n  deleting a line loses nothing, delete it. Skip any scene that does not earn a line.\n{shape_draft}\n- Causal glue between lines: But, So, Because of that, And soon, Now, Still. Never\n  write \"then\" or \"and then\" inside a line; a row of gestures is a storyboard, not a story.\n  Say WHY one thing leads to the next (\"she teleports back for it, but...\").\n- Time jumps get one or two sentences with the source's real interval:\n  \"By <age>, news arrives of <what happened> <real interval> earlier.\"\n- Friend voice: plain current English, warm, a little wry. Call the lead \"our guy\" or\n  \"our boy\" now and then, and a pitied loser \"the poor guy\". Sparingly, never twice\n  in a row. A rare short jab is fine (\"Yeah, great start.\"); it is optional.\n- Lines run about 20-30 words and flow into each other; mix in a short punch line.\n\nContinuity (the viewer must never lose the thread):\n- When you skip ahead, mark the jump in a few words, using only what the source shows:\n  \"Later that night\", \"Back at his place\", \"The next morning\". Never fuse two separate\n  moments into one sentence as if they happen together, and never move an event to a\n  place or time it did not happen.\n- Keep the source's order inside a scene; only an opening cold open may jump ahead, and\n  the line after it must make the step back obvious.\n- Refer to people the way EARLIER NARRATION did; never re-introduce someone the viewer\n  already knows beyond a few words, and never recap what was already told.\n- Pronouns need an obvious referent: name the person when two of the same gender are\n  in play or the speaker changes.\n\nNever bend these:\n- Facts, thoughts, history and motives must be supported by the plan. Do not add\n  physical specifics (contact, posture, anatomy) to illustrate a sentence.\n- Keep narrator knowledge apart from character knowledge: the narrator knowing an\n  identity does not mean a confused character recognizes it yet.\n- Keep intervals: months after an event is not the same day. Do not infer habits or\n  \"the first time in years\" from a calendar gap.\n- Attribute a reported/offscreen mechanism (he remembers, she says); never present it\n  as seen, and never replace it with a different invented one.\n- Self-blame is a belief, not proof of guilt. A future preview is not current action.\n- Dialogue is evidence of a reveal or decision, not a script to dub. Quote only when\n  the words ARE the turn.\n- A jab is ONLY a reaction: no new plot fact, skill, history or event in that slot.\n- No camera or media words (panel, page, chapter, scene, webtoon).\n- {delivery}\n\nSOURCE CHECKING:\n{transcript}\n\nReply strict JSON only with an unspoken causal outline and narration steps:\n{{\"outline\":[{{\"want\":\"grounded desire or empty\",\"obstacle\":\"grounded obstacle or empty\",\n\"choice\":\"grounded decision/revelation or empty\",\"change\":\"grounded consequence or empty\"}}],\n\"steps\":[{{\"beat\":\"snake_case story turn\",\"line\":\"spoken narration\",\"jab\":\"optional brief reaction or empty\",\"scene\":1}}]}}\nEvery step requires \"scene\": the integer of the plan moment it tells. You may skip\nmoments that do not earn a line, and a moment may take several steps sharing its\nscene and beat. Related sentences share a beat; a gesture alone is not a new beat.\nEach line is ONE spoken unit, HARD MAXIMUM 32 words; a jab at most 12 words.\nNo panel tags or holds. Outline fields stay empty when unsupported.\n";

export const STORY_REVIEW_TASK = "Independently assess this draft as a spoken recap AND against its sources.\nYou are not its author. Do not approve merely because it is fluent.\nSOURCE EVENTS AND CAST:\n{source}\nSOURCE FACT LOOKUP:\n{transcript}\nSTORY SO FAR (series memory; continuity only):\n{so_far}\nEARLIER NARRATION (continuity only):\n{previous}\nALREADY SPOKEN OPENING:\n{opening}\nFALLIBLE DRAFT (outline is unspoken; steps are spoken):\n{draft}\n\nThe draft is a SELECTIVE retelling by design. Skipped scenes, skipped reactions,\nskipped first appearances, merged exchanges and compressed time jumps are CORRECT\nwhen the story still makes sense. Never reject for coverage.\n\nReject only for these kinds:\n- caption_tour: a line describes what the picture shows (bodies, faces, gestures,\n  clothing, scenery) instead of what happens; OR a storyboard walk, a run of lines\n  chained with \"then\" that tours gestures in page order without causal glue.\n- causal_omission: ONLY a missing pivotal cause or payoff, without which the spoken\n  story is wrong or makes no sense (why the fight happens, what the reveal is, the\n  outcome). Not a missing scene, reaction, exchange, first appearance or quote. If\n  the narration says it in plain words, it is covered.\n- unsupported_fact: a fact, figure, motive, mechanism or history the sources do not\n  support; a reported/offscreen event told as literally seen; a character credited\n  with knowledge they have not acquired; self-blame turned into objective guilt.\n- continuity: a false interval or order (verify each temporal clause: the day,\n  before/after, since, durations, reading adjacent dialogue together); two separate\n  moments fused as if simultaneous, or an event moved to the wrong place/time; a skip\n  in time/place the listener cannot follow; a pronoun with no clear referent; a future\n  preview told as current action; earlier-episode facts imported into this one; or\n  an opening that re-tells what EARLIER NARRATION already told.\n{shape_review}- jab_fact: a jab that carries a new plot fact instead of a reaction.\nReader events and visual summaries are FALLIBLE: do not infer a separate incident or\ntimeline because different rows describe parts of one sequence; prefer original\ndialogue. Concrete action that changes the story is GOOD, not a caption tour.\nJudge the NARRATION, not the outline. Never rewrite it here.\nReply STRICT JSON with exactly these fields:\n{{\"verdict\":\"accept or reject\",\"assessment\":\"specific overall rationale\",\n\"issues\":[{{\"step\":1,\"kind\":\"caption_tour or causal_omission or unsupported_fact or continuity or jab_fact\",\n\"quote\":\"exact offending draft quote, or exact source quote for an omission\",\n\"reason\":\"specific explanation grounded in the source\"}}]}}\nUse the EXPLICIT step field on narration rows. Step indices are 1-based; 0 is a\nwhole-draft issue. A non-omission quote must occur EXACTLY in that row's line/jab.\nFor causal_omission quote the exact missing fact from SOURCE FACT LOOKUP or SOURCE\nEVENTS AND CAST, not your paraphrase. Accept iff issues is empty.\nAn unavailable/malformed review is not approval. Do not give a repair plan or new draft.\n";

