  "\"arc_summary\":\"the movie so far through this block, under 900 characters\",\n" +
  "\"open_threads\":[\"...\"]}}";

// ---------- OUTRO (editable default; native v2 addition, no v1 cache depends on it) ----------
export const DEFAULT_OUTRO_BRIEF = "You are writing the closing narration for a manhwa recap episode.\nThe series is {series}. It has {totalChapters} chapters so far, and this episode covered up to\nchapter {coveredChapters}. Write the outro as the same narrator who told the story: 2-4 short\nspoken sentences, 10-22 words each, in plain current English.\n\nWHAT IT DOES:\n- Remind the viewer where the story stands without re-summarising the episode.\n- Point at what is still unresolved, using only facts the series has already shown.\n- Tell them the next step plainly. Default call to action: \"{hook}\" - if the story is only\n  partly covered, ask them to comment for Part 2; if the series continues, name the next\n  chapter; if it ends, point at another series. Never invent a release date or a promise\n  the pages do not support.\n\nRULES:\n- No media words (panel, page, chapter, scene, story, video, webtoon) unless naming the\n  chapter number itself, and no \"in this video\" or \"thanks for watching\".\n- No robotic sign-off, no recap list, no question to the viewer beyond the call to action.\n- No new plot facts. Every name and number must exist in the series bible or the transcript.\n- No em-dashes. Use a comma, a full stop, or \"and\".\n\nOutput STRICT JSON only:\n{{\"lines\":[\"<spoken outro line>\", ...]}}";

// ---------- SCOUT ----------
export const SCOUT_CHAPTER = "You are choosing where a comic recap series starts. Below are its first\nchapters: the characters introduced and the story events in order, numbered per chapter.\n\n{chapters}\n\nPick the ONE event that makes the strongest episode 1 opening: the moment that makes a\nstranger need to know what happens next, and that makes sense without anything before it.\nJudge by the story's own charge, not by spectacle. Prefer the earliest chapter that carries it.\n\nOutput STRICT JSON only:\n{{\"chapter\":\"<chN>\",\"event\":<that event's number within its chapter>,\n\"why\":\"<one line: what makes a stranger need the next minute>\"}}";

export const SCOUT_PANEL = "You are opening a comic recap series. The opening moment, from {ch}:\n\nMOMENT: {scene}\n\nBelow are candidate panels of {ch}, each labeled with its id before the image. Look at them.\n\nPick 2-4 panels that carry this moment's charge, in the order your opening line reaches them -\nthe video cuts between them while the line is spoken. Judge by what you see: the pictures that\nland the stakes in one look. Never a panel that is mostly text or a promo.\n\nThen write the opening line, the first ~15 seconds of the video: a PITCH, not a replay of the\nscene. Spoken TO the viewer in casual current words: who this person is, the twist that throws\ntheir life off course, and the catch that makes the viewer stay. Short, punchy, grounded only in\nwhat the pages show. No \"in this video\". Put each panel's tag right after the words it illustrates.\n\nOutput STRICT JSON only:\n{{\"panels\":[\"<2-4 panel ids in line order>\"],\n\"hook\":\"<the opening line with each @tag right after the words it illustrates>\",\n\"introduces\":[\"<names the line establishes>\"]}}";

// ---------- PANEL MATCHER ----------
export const EVENT_MAP_BLOCK = "EVENT MAP (OPTIONAL, GUESSED HEURISTIC CONTEXT - NOT factual evidence). The reader's ordered\nlist of what it thinks happens in this chapter, with a PAGE RANGE per event that code estimated by\ncaption overlap. Both the ranges and the order are FALLIBLE GUESSES: they are neither proof of what\na page shows nor a chronological constraint. Your narration may flash back or reorder the chapter\nfreely, and this list does not force your lines to climb the pages. Treat it only as a soft hint for\nwhere a line's artwork might sit - the picture still has to actually depict what the line says (the\naction, the face, the object), and a line that fits no event is free to take any page. A panel where\nsomeone merely talks about it is not evidence.\n{events}\n";

export const MATCH_TASK = "You are the picture editor of a comic recap video. The narration is already\nwritten. For each numbered line, choose the panels to show while it is spoken - the artwork that\nfits what the line says. The video cuts between a line's panels while it plays.\n\nNARRATION ({ch}):\n{lines}\n\nPANELS of {ch}, grouped by page in reading order - \"p43:\" opens a page and the panels listed\nunder it are that page's panels in the order the eye moves across it, so neighbours you may run\nthrough sit next to each other (id | art score | where its speech bubble sits | what the artwork\nshows):\n{panels}\n{events_block}\nRules:\n- Lines grouped under the same `## BEAT` header belong to ONE scene turn. Keep a beat's panels\n  within that scene's visual sequence (progressing through its setup, arrival, reaction, exchange\n  and reveal) instead of jumping to an unrelated scene's pages mid-beat.\n- Match WHO does WHAT, to WHOM, in that scene. A shared character name, prop, background or a\n  panel where someone merely talks about the event is not action evidence.\n- A line's FIRST panel may come from ANYWHERE in the chapter, chosen by MEANING: the narration does\n  not follow page order, and what matters is only that the picture SHOWS what the line is about -\n  the action, the face, the object.\n- When more than one panel genuinely shows the line, take the HIGH-POINT picture over the quiet\n  one: a fight, a transformation, a reveal, a confrontation, a romantic beat, someone being\n  bullied or humiliated, a cold refusal. A panel that only mentions the moment is the weaker\n  pick even when it overlaps the line's words more.\n- Every panel AFTER that first one on the same line must be the panel that immediately FOLLOWS\n  it in reading order, continuing onto the next page when the page runs out. Never skip ahead\n  over a panel you left out, never go back, never reorder. So one line's panels are a single\n  unbroken slice of the chapter, and the slice may cross a page boundary.\n- Take only the contiguous panels that genuinely show the line's action - typically 1-3 panels.\n  A short line or single moment earns just one panel. Never pad a line with adjacent panels that\n  belong to the next event.\n- Each panel is used at most once in the whole video: a panel spent on one line is gone for the\n  rest, so spend runs where the picture earns them.\n- Prefer clean artwork: a bubble in a corner or at an edge is fine; a bubble at the centre or\n  covering the frame comes last, only when nothing else shows the moment.\n- When a continuation or internal-reaction line inside a beat introduces no new visual action\n  beyond the picture already on screen, return `[]` for that line so the video holds the active\n  scene instead of showing an unrelated panel.\n\nOutput STRICT JSON only:\n{{\"lines\":[{{\"n\":1,\"panels\":[\"<panel id>\", ...]}}, ...]}}";

/** Aliases used by the editable prompt stages; byte-identical to the v1 briefs. */
export const READER_BRIEF = READER_BATCH;
export const WRITER_BRIEF = WRITER_TASK;
export const STORY_WRITER_BRIEF = STORY_TASK;
export const PANEL_PICK_BRIEF = MATCH_TASK;

/** Stage prompt defaults, keyed by the editable prompt stage id. */
export function getDefaultBriefs(): Record<
  "reader" | "writer" | "story_writer" | "matcher" | "outro",
  string
> {
  return {
    reader: READER_BRIEF,
    writer: WRITER_BRIEF,
    story_writer: STORY_WRITER_BRIEF,
    matcher: PANEL_PICK_BRIEF,
    outro: DEFAULT_OUTRO_BRIEF,
  };
}
